  "verbalization": "Oxaprozin, a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID), is linked to Cyp2c9 through a shared pathway involving the class of NSAIDs. The evidence suggests that Oxaprozin, along with its metabolites Mefenamic Acid and Ketoprofen, binds to Cyp2c9, which is a key enzyme in the cytochrome P450 system. This binding is supported by the lowest common ancestor (LCA) relationships, where Oxaprozin and its metabolites share a common ancestor in the class of NSAIDs, which in turn shares a common ancestor with Cyp2c9. The presence of multiple NSAIDs in the pathway, all of which bind to Cyp2c9, indicates a general mechanism of interaction between NSAIDs and Cyp2c9, likely through shared metabolic pathways or enzyme inhibition. The integration of these pathways highlights a recurring mechanism of drug-target interaction, where the class of NSAIDs, through their common ancestry and binding to Cyp2c9, contributes to the observed relationship between Oxaprozin and Cyp2c9.",
